## Contracts with Type Parameters

```xiom
fn max[T: Ord](a: T, b: T) -> T
  ensures: result == a || result == b;
  ensures: result >= a && result >= b;
{ if a > b { return a; } return b; }
```

The contract applies regardless of whether T is Int, Float64, or a custom type.

### Contracts with Multiple Type Params
```xiom
fn zip_first[T, U](a: &Vec[T], b: &Vec[U]) -> (T, U)
  requires: a.len() > 0;
  requires: b.len() > 0;
{ return (a[0], b[0]); }
```

### Generic Constraints in Contracts
```xiom
fn find[T: Eq](items: &Vec[T], target: T) -> Option[Int]
  ensures: match result {
    Some(idx) => idx >= 0 && idx < items.len() && items[idx] == target,
    None => true,
  };
{
  for i in 0..items.len() {
    if items[i] == target { return Some(i); }
  }
  return None();
}
```

### Where Contracts Shine
Without contracts, generic functions are hard to trust. With contracts, they're verified for every instantiation.

